WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.337
A customer at the corner shop buys a teapot as a present.

2
00:00:06.337 --> 00:00:12.079
The shopkeeper puts it in a box, wraps the box in gift paper, and ties a ribbon around it.

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:20.360
Then the rider from Pedal Post, the town's bike courier, weighs it, scans the label, and rides off, quite unmoved by the bow.

4
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.617
A wrapped parcel is still a parcel.

5
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:26.323
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:26.323 --> 00:00:32.144
If each layer of wrapping just passes the parcel on, does it matter what order the layers go in?

7
00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:34.327
We'll answer that with real numbers.

8
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.473
The shop charges for every parcel it sends.

9
00:00:38.473 --> 00:00:40.188
0.50 for each kilometer.

10
00:00:40.188 --> 00:00:41.189
2.00 to start.

11
00:00:41.189 --> 00:00:44.048
3.00 more for a parcel over 5 kilograms.

12
00:00:44.048 --> 00:00:46.335
And never more than 8.00 in all.

13
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:50.281
The easy way is one big method that knows all four rules.

14
00:00:50.281 --> 00:00:55.689
But these rules change often, and every change means opening that one method again.

15
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:02.051
Wouldn't it be nice if each rule were a piece we could add, remove, or move, without touching the others?

16
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:04.654
That's the gift wrap again.

17
00:01:04.654 --> 00:01:08.717
Give each rule its own small layer that wraps the rule before it.

18
00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:12.155
In the middle sits the parcel: the price per kilometer.

19
00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:14.842
Around it, a layer that adds 2.00 to start.

20
00:01:14.842 --> 00:01:16.530
Then one for heavy parcels.

21
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.217
And the cap on the outside.

22
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:19.266
Let's map it.

23
00:01:19.266 --> 00:01:22.468
The parcel in the middle is the first rule, perKm.

24
00:01:22.468 --> 00:01:24.838
Each sheet of paper is one more rule.

25
00:01:24.838 --> 00:01:28.169
And the courier is the code that asks for the price.

26
00:01:28.169 --> 00:01:34.445
It only talks to the outside, because every layer answers the same one question: what do you cost?

27
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.414
Let's watch one question travel.

28
00:01:37.414 --> 00:01:40.364
A 12 kilogram parcel, going 20 kilometers.

29
00:01:40.364 --> 00:01:44.859
The question goes in, layer by layer, all the way to the middle.

30
00:01:44.859 --> 00:01:47.247
20 kilometers at 0.50 makes 10.00.

31
00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:50.639
On the way back out, each layer adds its rule.

32
00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:52.431
Plus 2.00 to start: 12.00.

33
00:01:52.431 --> 00:01:54.638
It's heavy, so plus 3.00: 15.00.

34
00:01:54.638 --> 00:01:57.120
And the cap says: no more than 8.00.

35
00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.913
So the courier hears 8.00.

36
00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:00.264
Your turn.

37
00:02:00.264 --> 00:02:03.331
Two more parcels, both going 4 kilometers.

38
00:02:03.331 --> 00:02:06.544
One weighs 2 kilograms, the other weighs 12.

39
00:02:06.544 --> 00:02:08.297
What does each one cost?

40
00:02:08.297 --> 00:02:09.465
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:15.868
The light one: 2.00 plus 4 times 0.50, so 4.00.

42
00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:18.552
The heavy one also pays the 3.00: 7.00.

43
00:02:18.552 --> 00:02:23.576
Neither one reaches 8.00, so the cap lets both answers through untouched.

44
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.767
Here it is in code.

45
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:31.168
Each rule is a small method that returns a new price, wrapped around the old one.

46
00:02:31.168 --> 00:02:34.369
In Java, it's a default method on the interface.

47
00:02:34.369 --> 00:02:36.569
In Kotlin, an extension function.

48
00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:42.304
Read the chain from the inside out: perKm is the parcel, and each call adds one layer.

49
00:02:42.533 --> 00:02:46.331
The tricky part is the bare cents of d, inside the layer.

50
00:02:46.331 --> 00:02:49.663
It means: first, ask the price I'm wrapped around.

51
00:02:49.663 --> 00:02:51.196
Then apply my one rule.

52
00:02:51.196 --> 00:02:53.461
The price inside is never changed.

53
00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:56.821
Now, back to our question.

54
00:02:56.821 --> 00:03:01.745
Let's move the cap one place in, so it sits inside the heavy parcel rule.

55
00:03:01.745 --> 00:03:04.713
Both chains compile, and both read sensibly.

56
00:03:04.713 --> 00:03:07.951
Does anything change, for the same heavy parcel?

57
00:03:07.951 --> 00:03:09.031
Pause and guess.

58
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.578
Watch the way out.

59
00:03:13.578 --> 00:03:14.037
10.00.

60
00:03:14.037 --> 00:03:15.491
Plus 2.00 is 12.00.

61
00:03:15.491 --> 00:03:18.399
Now the cap comes first: down to 8.00.

62
00:03:18.399 --> 00:03:22.303
And then the heavy parcel rule lands on top: 11.00.

63
00:03:23.233 --> 00:03:23.586
Well.

64
00:03:23.586 --> 00:03:29.374
That's 1,100 cents, for a parcel the shop promised would never cost more than 800.

65
00:03:29.374 --> 00:03:33.963
The same four pieces, two of them swapped, and a different price.

66
00:03:34.867 --> 00:03:38.479
A cap on the inside should really be called capped, for now.

67
00:03:38.479 --> 00:03:42.453
It caps what it sees, and every layer outside it still gets a say.

68
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:45.485
Jokes aside, the gift wrap predicted this.

69
00:03:45.485 --> 00:03:48.170
A ribbon tied under the paper is hidden.

70
00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.452
The outer layer has the last word.

71
00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:53.229
And here the picture breaks.

72
00:03:53.229 --> 00:03:54.492
Paper can only add.

73
00:03:54.492 --> 00:03:57.950
A layer of code can also take away, as the cap does.

74
00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:01.740
It could even decide not to ask the parcel inside at all.

75
00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:03.543
Now the name.

76
00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:12.376
An object with the same interface as the object it wraps, which does one extra thing and passes the call on, is called a decorator.

77
00:04:12.376 --> 00:04:14.399
Each rule here is a decorator.

78
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.672
Decorators wrap behavior too, not just arithmetic.

79
00:04:18.672 --> 00:04:25.145
Big Blue Vans has a booking line that's busy twice, and then books, as booking lines tend to be.

80
00:04:25.145 --> 00:04:28.854
One decorator, Logging, writes down every call it sees.

81
00:04:28.854 --> 00:04:31.753
Another, Retrying, tries up to three times.

82
00:04:31.967 --> 00:04:34.206
Put them together, in both orders.

83
00:04:34.206 --> 00:04:36.906
How many log lines does each order write?

84
00:04:36.906 --> 00:04:37.960
Pause and guess.

85
00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:46.854
With Retrying outside, Logging sits inside the loop, and sees every attempt: 3 lines.

86
00:04:46.854 --> 00:04:51.835
With Logging outside, it sees only the one booking that came back: 1 line.

87
00:04:51.835 --> 00:04:52.979
Neither is wrong.

88
00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:55.133
They answer different questions.

89
00:04:56.033 --> 00:05:06.420
The JDK has stacked decorators like this for decades: a DataOutputStream, around a BufferedOutputStream, around a GZIPOutputStream, around a file.

90
00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:10.831
Each one adds one thing: typed writes, buffering, compression.

91
00:05:11.733 --> 00:05:12.776
So, three rules.

92
00:05:12.776 --> 00:05:16.817
One: the order of the layers is behavior, so pin it in a test.

93
00:05:16.817 --> 00:05:20.467
Two: keep each layer to one extra thing, so it can move.

94
00:05:20.467 --> 00:05:25.029
Three: when a layer takes away, like the cap, ask what comes after it.

95
00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:28.726
So, does the order of the wrapping matter?

96
00:05:28.726 --> 00:05:28.992
Yes.

97
00:05:28.992 --> 00:05:33.512
Every layer passes the call on, but the outer one has the last word.

98
00:05:33.512 --> 00:05:37.501
The same four pieces gave 8.00 one way, and 11.00 the other.

99
00:05:37.733 --> 00:05:38.501
Quick check.

100
00:05:38.501 --> 00:05:42.468
Say the log feeds a chart of how many bookings the shop makes.

101
00:05:42.468 --> 00:05:44.004
Which order do you want?

102
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.778
Logging outside: one line per booking.

103
00:05:49.778 --> 00:05:55.341
To see how often the line is busy, put it inside, where both busy signals show up.
